   "kind": 1,
   "value": "**Task 3 result (the O(eps) layer, diagnostics; no closed-form fitting).** Top-kill: T = delta^Lambda s <v2 (x) v2|Psi>/<x0|Psi> is the (w1,y_A)-vacuum det6/Psi_Q (no det2) x top normalization: identity to 2.5e-82 over the 10 branches (vacuum Baxter residual 9.5e-194). T ~ eps^-6 for every branch (pure contamination: <v2 (x) v2|HW> = 0 for non-top HW kets); T/K = 2.30137 ([6,1,1]) and -1.24138 ([5,3,0]) are O(1), <= 4e-8 for all other branches (so the top-kill matters only at the clean [6,1,1], [5,3,0] and, at relative O(eps), the [5,2,1] pair).\n\n**(A) [5,2,1] x 2: the (2,1,1) weight, one direction at eps^0.** Convention: u = e1.E13 and u' = e1.E12.E23 on the two GT indices of (2,1,1) (u = (-1,8), u' = (2,8)); phi_(2,1,1)/delta^Lambda = A_u u + A_u' u' with A_u = O(eps), A_u'(0) = a'0 c'0/2 =: fac0; R_c := (u.C_c)/(u'.C_c), C_c = [P_HW Psi_c]_((2,1,1), e1), eps-Richardson. Exact (rational, at phi, om1 in {3/4,-5/3,7/2}): A_u'^(1)/fac0 = -(113/140) om2 + const, A_u^(1)/fac0 = (59/70) om2 + const, om1-independent, exact straight lines (residual 0). F = eps^4 (R_C - T)/(delta^Lambda fac0) = F0 + eps F1 + ..., g = F1/F0:\n\n| quantity | copy 1 | copy 2 |\n|---|---|---|\n| F0 spread over 12 om (om-independence) | 2.2e-17 | 4.7e-17 |\n| g(om2) a straight line: residual | <= 2.5e-11 | <= 2.5e-11 |\n| om1-dependence (slope diff, om1 = 7/2 vs 3/4) | 0 (1e-166) | 0 (1e-167) |\n| slope at phi (8 directions: spread) | -1.90107065557 (1.0e-11) | 0.97351289979 (6e-12) |\n| R_c = (u.C)/(u'.C), phi-independent | -0.8921886416 | 1.2040698297 |\n| slope_sig = -113/140 + (59/70) R_c | -1.5591304265 | 0.2077159993 |\n| **Delta_c = slope - slope_sig** (8 phi: spread) | **-0.34194022909** (6e-12) | **0.76579690047** (6e-12) |\n\nF0 itself (the CG functional u'.C) varies with phi (e.g. -1325.40 / -334.33 at phi, -280.13 / -70.66 at phi'), and so do the intercepts. Delta_1 + Delta_2 = 0.42385667137. slope, R_c and Delta_c are structural (eps-, om1-, om2-, phi-independent); only F0 and the intercept carry phi.\n\n**(B) F0 = 0 branches: K/S is a constant over eps, om, phi** (3 eps x 18 om; 8 phi at om A; variation is O(eps)):\n\n| branch | slice weight, leading order | K/S (eps -> 0, Richardson) | spread over 3 eps x 18 om | spread over 8 phi (eps 1e-6) | R_nu = C[idx2]/C[idx1] (phi / phi') |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [4,4,0] | (1,3,0), eps^1 | **0.38571428571 = 27/70** (kappa = 97/70) | 8.5e-6 | 3.1e-7 | - |\n| [4,3,1] #1 | (1,2,1), eps^1 | 0.41056992897 | 2.2e-5 | 2.7e-7 | -0.0789657625 / -0.0789657625 |\n| [4,3,1] #2 | (1,2,1), eps^1 | -0.01328651383 | 7.3e-4 | 2.2e-5 | -8.7671880836 / -8.7671880836 |\n| [4,2,2] | (1,1,2), eps^1 | 0.36610169492 = 108/295 | 1.8e-5 | 2.0e-6 | -1/3 exactly / -1/3 |\n| [3,3,2] | (0,2,2), eps^2 | 3.65080357143 | 1.8e-5 | 6.6e-7 | - |\n\nR_nu convention: GT-index basis, u = unit covector on the first GT index of the weight, u' on the second; R_nu is phi-independent (differences 1e-20). The [4,3,1] pair has copy-dependent constants (as the adjoint octets, where tau_c entered); [4,4,0] is a rational at P1 (27/70 = theta12 - h). The clean branches [6,1,1], [5,3,0] have K/S = lam eps with lam om- and phi-dependent (lam = 0.10970 and -0.00789 at om A, phi): their O(eps) layer needs the same slope machinery as (A) (not done here; single-direction weights, so no mixing R).\n",
